    "content": "Tennessee State University (TSU) is a premier public, historically black land-grant university (HBCU) in Nashville, Tennessee, founded in 1912. TSU offers comprehensive undergraduate and graduate programs, with notable recognition in music business and agriculture. The university is known for its \"Aristocrat of Bands,\" competitive NCAA Division I sports, and a vibrant campus life. Key Information Location: Nashville, Tennessee. Enrollment: Approximately 6,310 (Fall 2024). Acceptance Rate: Approximately 60-70%. Athletics: TSU Tigers compete in the Ohio Valley Conference (OVC). Tuition: Approximately $8,616 for in-state and $22,416 for out-of-state students. Academic and Campus Life Programs: TSU is a land-grant institution focusing on teaching, research, and service. It offers a wide range of degrees through various colleges. Heritage: As an HBCU, it holds a rich tradition and is a member of the Thurgood Marshall College Fund. Distinctions: Recognized by Billboard as a top music business school. Recent Developments Sports: The TSU men's basketball team qualified for the 2026 NCAA Tournament, facing Iowa State in the first round. Academics: The university frequently holds spring preview days for prospective students to engage with academic programs. Infrastructure: The university has worked to address previous on-campus housing challenges to accommodate its student population. For more information, you can visit the official Tennessee State University website.",
